1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:12,232
The clock period has to cover the slowest route from one register to the next.

2
00:00:12,332 --> 00:00:14,894
Today we use that rule backwards.

3
00:00:14,894 --> 00:00:18,848
If a route is too slow, we change the route.

4
00:00:20,083 --> 00:00:22,347
Here is a piece of a processor.

5
00:00:22,347 --> 00:00:28,239
Two numbers come out of a register, an adder adds them, a shifter shifts the sum,

6
00:00:28,239 --> 00:00:31,367
and the result goes into a second register.

7
00:00:31,467 --> 00:00:38,293
For example three plus one is four, and shifted left by one bit that becomes eight.

8
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:44,146
For the timing, say the adder takes five nanoseconds and the shifter three,

9
00:00:44,146 --> 00:00:49,328
and each register has a clock to q delay of one and a setup time of one.

10
00:00:49,428 --> 00:00:52,426
The only route goes through everything,

11
00:00:52,426 --> 00:00:58,346
so the period is one plus five plus three plus one, which is ten nanoseconds.

12
00:00:58,783 --> 00:01:05,395
At every edge a new pair goes in on the left, and the previous result is caught on the right.

13
00:01:05,495 --> 00:01:08,722
So we get one result every ten nanoseconds,

14
00:01:08,722 --> 00:01:13,825
and each result takes ten nanoseconds from going in to being caught.

15
00:01:14,283 --> 00:01:16,703
Suppose that is not fast enough.

16
00:01:16,703 --> 00:01:20,827
We can't make the adder or the shifter any quicker.

17
00:01:20,927 --> 00:01:23,263
But notice why the period is long.

18
00:01:23,263 --> 00:01:29,585
It is long only because one signal must get through both of them between two edges.

19
00:01:30,016 --> 00:01:32,150
So let's stop asking for that.

20
00:01:32,150 --> 00:01:36,600
Put a third register between the adder and the shifter.

21
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:43,330
Now the sum is caught in the middle at one edge, and the shifter works on it during the next period.

22
00:01:43,783 --> 00:01:47,469
Each period only has to cover one of the two halves.

23
00:01:47,469 --> 00:01:52,740
The first half is one plus five plus one, seven nanoseconds,

24
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:56,957
and the second is one plus three plus one, five.

25
00:01:57,057 --> 00:02:02,623
One clock drives all the registers, so the period has to suit the slower half.

26
00:02:02,623 --> 00:02:06,795
That gives seven nanoseconds instead of ten.

27
00:02:08,016 --> 00:02:09,778
Watch the data move.

28
00:02:09,778 --> 00:02:14,080
At the first edge the pair three and one goes in.

29
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:18,376
At the second edge its sum, four, is caught in the middle.

30
00:02:18,376 --> 00:02:24,722
And at that same edge a new pair goes in on the left, because the adder is free again.

31
00:02:25,183 --> 00:02:29,783
At the third edge the first result, eight, is caught on the right.

32
00:02:29,783 --> 00:02:34,611
The second sum moves to the middle, and a third pair goes in.

33
00:02:34,711 --> 00:02:38,734
From then on a finished result comes out at every edge,

34
00:02:38,734 --> 00:02:43,709
and two computations are always under way at once, one in each half.

35
00:02:44,950 --> 00:02:47,344
Now compare the two designs.

36
00:02:47,344 --> 00:02:54,486
Before, we got one result every ten nanoseconds, and now we get one every seven.

37
00:02:54,586 --> 00:03:01,466
The number of results per second is called the throughput, and it has gone up by more than forty percent.

38
00:03:01,916 --> 00:03:04,118
But follow one single pair.

39
00:03:04,118 --> 00:03:10,340
It needs two periods to get through, and two times seven is fourteen nanoseconds,

40
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:12,414
where before it needed ten.

41
00:03:12,514 --> 00:03:18,754
The time one item takes from start to finish is called the latency, and it got worse.

42
00:03:19,216 --> 00:03:21,514
It got worse for two reasons.

43
00:03:21,514 --> 00:03:28,872
The item now passes an extra register and pays that register's clock to q and setup time.

44
00:03:28,972 --> 00:03:33,891
And the faster half sits idle for two nanoseconds in every period,

45
00:03:33,891 --> 00:03:37,170
because the clock is set by the slower half.

46
00:03:37,616 --> 00:03:39,904
This move is called pipelining.

47
00:03:39,904 --> 00:03:44,112
It is the right trade when you care about results per second,

48
00:03:44,112 --> 00:03:48,112
as a processor running a long stream of instructions does.

49
00:03:48,212 --> 00:03:53,890
It is the wrong one when you only care how soon a single answer comes back.

50
00:03:55,116 --> 00:03:59,015
Step back and look at the three circuits of this series,

51
00:03:59,015 --> 00:04:03,540
the running sum, the machine that counts ones, and this pipeline.

52
00:04:03,640 --> 00:04:05,852
They are all built the same way.

53
00:04:05,852 --> 00:04:10,569
There are blocks of logic with no memory, separated by registers,

54
00:04:10,569 --> 00:04:15,140
and a clock that connects to the registers and to nothing else.

55
00:04:15,583 --> 00:04:23,239
Sometimes a register's output loops back, as in the first two, and sometimes the data only moves forward.

56
00:04:23,239 --> 00:04:28,309
Registers can sit back to back, and so can logic blocks.

57
00:04:28,409 --> 00:04:35,561
That is the whole model of a synchronous digital system, and a processor is a large one of these.

